{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "5357273089",
    "title": "PRD: Non-Food Pantry Ordering for IKCs",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://wonder.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SC/pages/5357273089",
    "space": {
      "key": "SC",
      "name": "Supply Chain"
    },
    "version": 30,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Links** | |\n| **Epics** | *TBD* |\n| **Tech Design** | *TBD, to be linked per system* |\n| **Figma Links** | *TBD (Pantry Web vs. Mobile ordering surface; see Open Questions)* |\n| **Test / Launch Plan** | Included below as part of PRD |\n\n| **Reviewer** | **Role** | **Date Reviewed** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Pavel Romanov | Finance / Systems & Data | Requires physical signature |\n| *TBD additional Finance & Accounting stakeholder* | Finance / Accounting |  |\n| Camden Hill | Product (Supply Chain Systems) |  |\n| Bob Zhuang | Pantry Eng |  |\n| Sahil Vora | Platform and Sporklift Eng |  |\n| Sadhana Subramanian | Supply Chain Catalog Product |  |\n| Paul Farina | Supply Chain Catalog Dev |  |\n| *TBD* | Procurement |  |\n| Dave Prochnow | DISH Ops |  |\n| Steve Vincent | IKC Ops |  |\n| Sim Taylor | Planning / VDC (vendor → DISH) |  |\n| Jonny Li | Data |  |\n\n14truenonelisttrue\n\n---\n\n1. Project Overview\n===================\n\n### Executive Summary\n\nFor a subset of non-food items (smallwares, packaging, supplies), Wonder can achieve **materially better unit economics by buying in bulk through DISH and then replenishing the IKCs routinely** (daily/recurring), rather than having each IKC order direct from the vendor.\n\nToday, the GMs of our IKCs place orders for most of their non-food items through the **Ed Don** (Edward Don & Company) vendor ordering portal. There are some other minor suppliers but they are out of scope for now. The order lands as a direct third-party purchase that bypasses Wonder's distribution network and bulk pricing.\n\nThis project moves the **DISH-eligible** subset of those items off the Ed Don portal and into **Pantry**, where GMs select how much of each SKU to order. DISH carries the bulk inventory (managed in ShipHero), and Pantry orders draw down DISH stock on a routine cadence. The remaining items continue to be ordered directly through Ed Don.\n\n**An item is orderable through the Ed Don order guide** ***XOR*** **through Pantry.** Sourcing is mutually exclusive and is determined centrally by the Supply Chain Catalog. When an item is migrated to DISH-sourcing, it should be removed from the Ed Don order guide in the same change so GMs cannot double-order it.\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n* **Cost:** Per-IKC direct vendor ordering forgoes the volume pricing Wonder can negotiate by consolidating demand at DISH. For high-volume non-food SKUs, the delta is significant enough to justify carrying the inventory centrally.\n* **Operational fragmentation:** GMs order non-food through a separate vendor portal that is disconnected from Pantry, the system they already use for receiving and inventory. This is a second tool, a second login, and a second mental model.\n* **Reconciliation:** Direct Ed Don POs have historically created 3-way-match (PO → Invoice → Receipt) and SKU-mapping pain (see Ed Don Receiving PRD). Routing eligible demand through DISH brings these items into Wonder's own order/ledger pipeline (POMS → Sporklift → ShipHero), where they are governed and auditable.\n\n### Goals\n\n* Enable GMs to order **DISH-sourced non-food items through Pantry** by selecting quantities per SKU.\n* Enforce **mutually exclusive sourcing** (Ed Don order guide XOR Pantry) via the Supply Chain Catalog, so each item has exactly one ordering path at any time.\n* Stand up the **inventory ledger and order-data model** end-to-end (Pantry → POMS → Sporklift → ShipHero) for this class of items, with explicit Finance sign-off.\n* Capture the **cost savings** by consolidating purchasing at DISH and replenishing IKCs routinely.\n* Properly size demand from DISH to **enable accurate buys from supplier to DISH** (via VDC).\n\n### Non-goals (this phase)\n\n* Migrating *all* non-food items off Ed Don. Only the cost-justified, DISH-eligible subset moves in this phase; the rest stay on Ed Don.\n\n### High-level plan\n\n1. **Catalog & sourcing setup**: Supply Chain Catalog defines the DISH-eligible non-food items and sets their source to DISH; the same items are removed from the Ed Don order guide. This is the switch that guarantees one-and-only-one ordering path.\n2. **Vendor → DISH buying (VDC)**: Generate accurate buy recommendations from the vendor into DISH via VDC so the right quantities are on hand to fulfill IKC demand. See **Vendor → DISH Replenishment (VDC)** below.\n3. **DISH inventory**: ShipHero carries bulk non-food inventory at DISH (receiving, putaway, lot/expiration where applicable, on-hand as source of truth).\n4. **Pantry ordering**: GMs order quantities per SKU in Pantry (Web or Mobile, TBD). Orders are supplied from DISH.\n5. **Order management**: POMS understands and manages these orders through their lifecycle (PLANNED → PLACED → SHIPPED → RECEIVED).\n6. **Fulfillment integration**: Sporklift posts the orders to ShipHero and syncs status/inventory back to POMS.\n7. **Receiving**: IKC receives in Pantry against the order, closing the loop and feeding the ledger.\n\n### Why now, and what happens if we don’t do this?\n\nWe stand to save $130k-150k per month if we enact this. We cannot launch until SD1 due to space constraints, so we should target mid-September tech readiness.\n\n### Milestone Roadmap\n\n| Phase | Milestone | Date |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Discovery | PRD review + Pavel Romanov sign-off on ledger/order-data model | *TBD* |\n| Discovery | Per-system tech designs (Pantry, POMS, Sporklift, ShipHero, SCC, VDC) | *TBD* |\n| Build | * Catalog sourcing + Ed Don order-guide removal mechanism * Pantry ordering surface + POMS/Sporklift/ShipHero order flow * VDC buy recommendations vendor → DISH | *TBD* |\n| Pilot | Single IKC + limited SKU set, DISH-sourced via Pantry | *TBD* |\n| Rollout | Expand SKUs and IKCs | *TBD* |\n\n### Non-tech considerations\n\n* GM training on ordering DISH-sourced non-food in Pantry (and which items are no longer on Ed Don).\n* Procurement process to negotiate/commit bulk vendor pricing into DISH.\n* DISH ops capacity to store and routinely ship the added non-food volume.\n* Change comms so GMs understand why some items moved and where to find them.\n\n### Stakeholders and personas\n\n| Persona | Primary | Interest | Success Criteria |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| IKC GM | N/A | Order non-food easily in one tool; receive reliably | Orders DISH-sourced items in Pantry; no Ed Don double-ordering |\n| DISH / Warehouse | Dave Prochnow | Carry & ship bulk non-food accurately | Accurate DISH on-hand in ShipHero; routine IKC replenishment shipped on SLA |\n| Procurement | Jennifer Snyder | Decide eligible items; manage vendor vs. DISH sourcing | Clean order guide; correct source per item; bulk POs to vendor |\n| Finance / Accounting | Pavel Romanov | Auditable order & ledger data; 3-way match | Sign-off on ledger/order model; reconcilable receipts |\n| Product (Supply Chain Systems) | Camden Hill | Coherent cross-system design | Single ordering path per item; end-to-end data integrity |\n| Pantry Eng | Bob Zhuang | Ordering UI + order/ledger integration | GMs order & receive in Pantry |\n| POMS / Sporklift / ShipHero Eng | Sahil Vora | Order lifecycle & fulfillment | Orders flow Pantry → POMS → Sporklift → ShipHero and back |\n| Supply Chain Catalog | Sadhana Subramanian | Item identity & sourcing rules | Mutually-exclusive source enforced centrally |\n| Planning / VDC (vendor → DISH) | Sim Taylor | Accurate demand signal to buy vendor → DISH | Right quantities bought into DISH; minimal over/under-stock |\n\n---\n\n2. Scope & Requirements\n=======================\n\nBackground\n----------\n\n### Pre-Requisites\n\n| Action | Owner | Expected Date | Links / Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Confirm additional Finance & Accounting reviewers | Camden Hill |  | Placeholder reviewers above |\n| Confirm Pantry ordering surface (Web vs. Mobile) | Anusha Chemicala |  | See Open Questions |\n| Per-system tech designs | System owners | *TBD* | Pantry, POMS, Sporklift, ShipHero, SCC, VDC |\n| Identify the DISH-eligible non-food SKU set + cost justification | Merchandising | *TBD* | The bulk-vs-direct savings analysis defines what moves |\n\n### Assumptions\n\n| Assumption | Confirmed By | Confirmed Date |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bulk DISH purchasing yields a net cost benefit vs. direct Ed Don for the selected SKUs | Eloy Olvera Mendoza |  |\n\nHigh-level system responsibilities\n----------------------------------\n\n* **Supply Chain Catalog:** Create items & determine sourcing\n* **Pantry:** Provide the ordering interface\n* **Platform:** Understand & manage the orders\n* **Sporklift:** Post orders & integrate with ShipHero\n* **ShipHero:** Manage DISH inventory\n* **Planning (VDC + MEP):** Forecast non-food demand + provide buys into DISH\n* **Planning (Ladle):** TBD depending on whether we create these as orders vs requests, and omit any non-DISH items from plans\n\nSKU strategy and routes\n-----------------------\n\nThe Supply Chain Catalog is the system of record for where an item can be ordered from. For any items that are going to be supplied by DISH, we must create a WSKU, and we must map vendor SKUs to WSKU UOMs. We will also introduce a route for DISH to IKC for those SKUs. In tandem, we will remove the SKUs from the Ed Don order guide.\n\n@Sadhana Subramanian to fill in details on item modeling via SCC and VC.\n\nPantry will only expose items that are on the DISH route as available for order.\n\nPantry interface and ordering logic\n-----------------------------------\n\n@Anusha Chemicala to fill this in pending discovery\n\nThis section to include functional requirements for interface, link to visual design, guardrails on order quantities, logic around count of orders users can place per day, etc.\n\nAdd user research notes to appendix.\n\nOverview\n--------\n\nGeneral Managers (GMs) at each IKC will manually place orders for DISH-sourced non-food items through the Pantry interface. The system operates on a **first-come, first-served** basis with real-time inventory allocation to prevent double-ordering across IKCs.\n\nOrdering Flow\n-------------\n\n### 1. Order Placement\n\n* **Who:** IKC General Managers\n* **When:** GMs can place orders at any time, multiple times per day\n* **How:** Manual selection of SKU quantities through Pantry (Web? SCP? TBD)\n* **Frequency:** Multiple orders per day are permitted, though ideally GMs will consolidate into a single daily order\n\n  + *Reporting will track number of orders per day per IKC to flag excessive ordering patterns*\n\n### 2. Order Cutoff and Delivery Timeline\n\n**Order Cutoff Time:** 2:00 PM daily *(this is an example, actual timing to be finalized with SCC/Delivery Schedule)*\n\n* **Orders placed before 2:00 PM:** Delivered the next business day\n* **Orders placed after 2:00 PM:** Delivered in 2 business days\n* **Example:** An order placed Monday at 3:00 PM will be delivered Wednesday\n\n### 3. Inventory Allocation (First-Come, First-Served)\n\n* When a GM submits an order, the requested quantity is **immediately reserved/allocated** from DISH inventory\n* This prevents double-allocation across multiple IKCs\n* **Example:**\n\n  + DISH has 18 cups in stock\n  + 9:00 AM: Upper West Side orders 10 cups → DISH inventory reduces to 8 cups (10 reserved for UWS)\n  + 10:00 AM: Chelsea attempts to order 10 cups → System recognizes only 8 cups available\n\n### 4. Order Editing and Cancellation\n\n* GMs can **edit or cancel orders** up until the order cutoff time for that delivery window\n* **Example:** An order placed Monday at 3:00 PM can be edited/cancelled up until Tuesday at 2:00 PM (the cutoff for Wednesday delivery)\n* When an order is cancelled, the reserved inventory is immediately released back to DISH available stock\n\nInformation Displayed to GMs\n----------------------------\n\nFor each non-food SKU, GMs will see:\n\n| Field | Description | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Item Name** | SKU display name | User-friendly name (e.g., \"12oz Paper Cups\") |\n| **Item Number** | Unique SKU identifier | To be determined: 9\\* or 42\\* numbering scheme |\n| **On Hand Quantity** | Current inventory at the IKC | Real-time available quantity at this location |\n| **Shipped Quantity** | Quantity currently in transit to this IKC | Orders that have shipped but not yet received |\n| **Expected Delivery Date** | When should the IKC expect the order | mm/dd/yy |\n| **DISH Inventory** | Available quantity at DISH warehouse | See \"DISH Inventory Display Options\" below |\n| **Type** | Item category | Examples: Guest Packaging, PPE, Cleaning Supplies, Smallwares |\n| **Restaurant(s)** | Which menu brands use this item | Helps prevent ordering wrong items for inactive brands |\n| **Cost** | per unit cost | we can show the total order cost to IKC’s for visibility if needed |\n\nDISH Inventory Display Options\n------------------------------\n\n*The team will align on which option to implement based on operational testing and GM feedback. Both options are presented below with trade-offs.*\n\n### Option A: Always Show Real-Time DISH On-Hand\n\nDisplay the current available DISH inventory for each SKU at all times, updating in real-time as orders are placed.\n\n#### ✓ Pros\n\n* **Full transparency:** GMs see exactly what's available before ordering\n* **Informed decisions:** GMs can adjust order quantities based on DISH stock levels\n* **No surprises:** No post-submission confirmation screens or order modifications needed\n* **Prevents futile orders:** GMs won't attempt to order items that are out of stock\n\n#### ✗ Cons\n\n* **Potential panic ordering:** GMs may over-order when they see low DISH inventory, creating artificial demand spikes\n* **Competitive behavior:** GMs may rush to place orders when inventory is low, rather than ordering based on actual need\n* **System complexity:** Requires real-time inventory sync and display updates as other IKCs place orders\n\n#### User Experience - Option A\n\n1. GM views item list and sees \"DISH: 8 cups available\"\n2. If GM tries to order more than available (e.g., 10 cups), the system either:\n\n   * Prevents submission with message: \"Only 8 cups available at DISH\"\n   * Or auto-adjusts quantity to maximum available (8 cups)\n3. If DISH inventory is 0, the item shows \"Out of Stock at DISH\" and cannot be ordered\n\n### Option B: Confirm Availability After Order Submission\n\nGMs place orders without seeing real-time DISH inventory. If DISH has insufficient stock, a confirmation screen displays the available quantity before finalizing the order.\n\n#### ✓ Pros\n\n* **Prevents panic ordering:** GMs order based on their actual needs, not DISH stock levels\n* **Simpler interface:** Less visual clutter; GMs focus on their own inventory needs\n* **Reduced system load:** No need for real-time DISH inventory display updates\n* **Natural demand signal:** Orders reflect true IKC needs rather than DISH supply constraints\n\n#### ✗ Cons\n\n* **Extra step:** GMs may need to confirm/adjust orders after submission if DISH is low on stock\n* **Potential frustration:** Repeated stockouts could frustrate GMs who don't know availability upfront\n* **Delayed awareness:** GMs only learn about stockouts after investing time in order creation\n\n#### User Experience - Option B\n\n1. GM selects items and quantities based on IKC needs (no DISH inventory shown)\n2. GM submits order\n3. **If DISH has sufficient inventory:** Order confirmed immediately, inventory allocated\n4. **If DISH has insufficient inventory:** Confirmation screen appears:\n\n   * \"You ordered 10 cups, but DISH only has 8 available. Confirm order for 8 cups?\"\n   * GM can confirm the reduced quantity or cancel the order\n5. **If DISH is completely out of stock:** Message appears before submission:\n\n   * \"Item X is currently out of stock at DISH and cannot be ordered at this time\"\n\nOpen Questions for Alignment\n----------------------------\n\n* **Ordering surface:** Will this be Pantry Web or should we build a net new tool that can live in Supply Chain Portal?\n* **Final order cutoff time:** Confirm 2:00 PM or adjust based on DISH operational constraints\n* **DISH inventory display:** Select Option A or Option B based on pilot testing and stakeholder feedback\n* **Out-of-stock fallback:** Should there be any mechanism to order from Ed Don if DISH is out of stock, or simply wait for DISH replenishment?\n\n---\n\n3. Data and Ledger Requirements\n===============================\n\n#DEEBFF\n\nThe following section outlines data collection and reporting. Areas to model explicitly and review with Finance & Accounting: DISH and Pantry inventory ledgers, order data model including 3-way match, cost & valuation, reporting/BRD.\n\nPantry Data\n-----------\n\n@Anusha Chemicala to fill in this section\n\n### Overview\n\nPantry will maintain a complete transaction ledger for all non-food inventory movements. Each transaction represents a single inventory event (receipt, consumption, adjustment) and will be logged with sufficient detail for operational reporting and financial reconciliation.\n\n### Ledger Structure\n\nThe following table structure will be used to log all non-food inventory transactions in Pantry:\n\n| Field | Type | Description | Example Values |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **TXN ID** | Integer | Unique transaction identifier, auto-incrementing | 1, 2, 3, ... |\n| **SKU** | String | Item identifier | 9001, 42001 |\n| **Batch** | String | Batch/lot identifier (if applicable) | -, BATCH-001 |\n| **Reason Code Category** | String | High-level transaction type | Add, Remove, Adjust |\n| **Reason** | String | Detailed reason for the transaction | Received, Used for Customer Order (equivalent of Cooked for food), Consumed for Standard Operations, Lost, Found, Missing, Received Damaged |\n| **Change** | Integer | Quantity change (positive or negative) | 1000, -2, -422 |\n| **Result** | Integer | Resulting on-hand quantity after transaction | 1000, 998, 0 |\n| **Notes** | String | Additional context or system-generated messages | can use this to capture free text from any receiving issues (i.e. product arrived crushed in transit) |\n| **Timestamp** | Datetime | When the transaction occurred | 2026-06-23 14:32:15 |\n| **User ID** | String | Who performed the transaction | achemicala@wonder.com, SYSTEM |\n| **IKC Name** | String | Which IKC this transaction belongs to | Upper West Side, Chelsea, etc. |\n\n### Transaction Types and Examples\n\n#### Example 1: BOM Item - Auto-Decrement on Customer Order (Does Not Reach Zero before Replenishment)\n\n*Scenario: 1 oz plastic sauce cups are part of the BOM for a Burger Baby Burger. Each burger order automatically decrements cup inventory.*\n\n| TXN ID | SKU | Reason Code | Reason | Change | Result | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 9001 | Add | Received | 1000 | 1000 |  |\n| 2 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -2 | 998 |  |\n| 3 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -1 | 997 |  |\n| 4-499 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -1 | 422 |  |\n| 500 | 9001 | Adjust | Consumed for Standard Operations | -422 | 0 |  |\n| 500 | 9001 | Add | Received | 2000 | 2000 |  |\n\n#### Example 2: BOM Item - Auto-Decrement, Reaches Zero Before Replenishment\n\n*Scenario: Inventory depletes to zero from customer orders before new shipment arrives. System should prevent negative inventory.*\n\n| TXN ID | SKU | Reason Code | Reason | Change | Result | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 9001 | Add | Received | 1000 | 1000 |  |\n| 2 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -2 | 998 |  |\n| 3 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -1 | 997 |  |\n| 4-799 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -1 | 0 | If we attempt to consume below zero (which should rarely happen), then reconcile inventory with an actual amount to that being consumed. |\n| 800 | 9001 | Adjust | Found | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 800 | 9001 | Remove | Used for Customer Order | -1 | 0 |  |\n\n#### Example 3: Non-BOM Item - Manual Consumption\n\n*Scenario: Cleaning supplies are not in any menu item BOM. GM manually adjusts inventory when supplies are used for standard operations.*\n\n| TXN ID | SKU | Reason Code | Reason | Change | Result | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 9001 | Add | Received | 1000 | 1000 | If not in menu item BOMs, then simply consume on hand qty and replenish to received amount upon new delivery. |\n| 2 | 9001 | Adjust | Consumed for Standard Operations | -1000 | 0 | System automatically records that cleaning supplies were consumed for standard operations. |\n| 2 | 9001 | Add | Received | 2000 | 2000 |  |\n\n### Transaction Reasons by Reason Code\n\n| **Reason Code Category** | **Reason** | **When It Occurs** | **Who Triggers** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Add** | Received | IKC receives a shipment from DISH and confirms receipt in Pantry | GM (manual) |\n| **Remove** | Used for Customer Order | A menu item containing this SKU in its BOM is prepared for a customer | System (automatic) |\n| **Adjust** | Consumed for Standard Operations | Non-BOM items used for operations (cleaning, prep, etc.); GM manually records consumption or System auto adjusts when a non-BOM item is received | GM (manual) or System (automatic) |\n| **Adjust** | Lost | Items lost after receiving; GM manually adjusts inventory downward | GM (manual) |\n| **Adjust** | Found | Unexpected stock discovered during inventory counts; GM manually adjusts inventory upward | GM (manual) |\n| **Adjust** | Missing | Items unaccounted for during receiving; GM adjusts inventory downward during receiving process | GM (manual) |\n| **Adjust** | Received Damaged | Items received but damaged/unusable; GM adjusts inventory downward during receiving process | GM (manual) |\n\n### BOM vs. Non-BOM Item Behavior\n\n| Aspect | BOM Items | Non-BOM Items |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Auto-Decrement** | ✓ Yes - automatically decremented when customer orders containing this item are fulfilled | ✗ No - inventory does not change on customer orders |\n| **Manual Consumption** | Only for adjustments/corrections outside of customer orders | GM can manually record when items are consumed |\n| **Examples** | 12oz cups, burger boxes, utensils included in menu items | Cleaning supplies, PPE, general-use containers not tied to specific menu items |\n| **Inventory Tracking** | System-driven based on customer order volume | GM-driven based on actual usage and new orders |\n\n### Reporting Requirements\n\nThe following reports will be generated from the Pantry ledger data:\n\n| Report | Purpose | Key Metrics |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Orders per Day per IKC** | Flag IKCs with excessive ordering behavior | Number of orders placed per IKC per day; identify outliers ordering >3x per day |\n| **Inventory Consumption by Category** | Understand how non-food is being used | Breakdown of Remove vs. Adjust transactions; identify high manual-consumption SKUs |\n| **Loss and Find Frequency** | Identify inventory accuracy issues | Frequency and magnitude of Loss/Find adjustments by IKC and SKU |\n| **Stockout Incidents** | Monitor DISH inventory levels and IKC stockouts | Number of times DISH had 0 inventory for a SKU; number of times IKC hit 0 on-hand |\n| **Receiving Issues** | Identify problem sku’s | Frequency and magnitude of missing items and items received damaged |\n\n### Data Validation Rules\n\n* **No negative inventory:** System must prevent transactions that would result in negative on-hand quantities (Result < 0)\n* **Mandatory fields:** All fields except Batch and Notes are required for every transaction\n* **Change sign validation:** Add transactions must have positive Change values; Remove transactions must have negative Change values; Adjust can be positive or negative\n* **Result calculation:** Result must equal previous Result + Change; system validates this on every transaction\n* **Timestamp sequencing:** Transactions must be timestamped in chronological order per SKU per IKC\n\n### Integration Points\n\n* **POMS:** Receives order data when GM submits an order in Pantry; updates order status (SHIPPED, RECEIVED)\n* **ShipHero:** Source of truth for DISH inventory levels; allocation/reservation happens in ShipHero when orders are placed\n* **Cookbook/SCC:** Determines which SKUs are BOM items and triggers auto-decrement on customer orders\n* **Reporting/Analytics:** Consumes ledger data for operational dashboards and spend analysis\n\nCurrent State Feedback from users here - UX Interview Learnings\n\nPlatform Data\n-------------\n\nDetails: POMS / Platform Requirements — DISH-Sourced Non-Food (Pantry → IKC)\n\n| **FR** | **Capability** | **Detail / fields** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| FR1: Ingest Pantry orders | Accept DISH→IKC non-food orders from Pantry (manual GM-directed order) | create PO with `order_type=CUSTOMER`, supplier=DISH, receiver=IKC, `planned_quantity` per line |\n| FR2: Lifecycle management | Manage PLANNED → PLACED → SHIPPED → RECEIVED + closure | `po.status`/`fulfillment_status`/`primary_status`, SLA timers, `closure.inbound/outbound` |\n| FR3: Dual-SKU on line | Persist supplier SKU + UOM SKU; no qty/UOM translation | `supplier_sku`, `uom_sku`; qty = packaged units |\n| FR4: Sourcing gate | Only allow orders for SCC source = DISH items | derive orderability from SCC; block Ed Don-sourced items and food items |\n| FR5: Fulfillment handoff | Post to Sporklift→ShipHero; sync shipment + status/inventory back | `shipment.*`, `shipped_quantity`, `received_quantity` |\n| FR6: Receiving & NR | Apply IKC receipts; roll shorts to NR; close PO | `received_quantity`, `not_received_quantity`, closure logic |\n| FR7: Ledger & reporting | Feed e2e PO table + unified ledger | `brd_poms_purchase_orders_e2e`, `int_ledger_purchase_orders` |\n\nVendor → DISH Replenishment (VDC)\n---------------------------------\n\n@Sim Taylor to add requirements\n\nConsiderations to work through (starting framework from Sim's review; to be expanded by Planning):\n\n* **Demand prediction (exploration):** Of the DISH-eligible SKUs, how many are tied to menu-item BOMs vs. needing to be estimated from customer orders / other signals?\n* **Forecasting BOM-linked non-food SKUs:** Extend MEP to forecast BOM-linked non-food SKUs (today MEP forecasts food SKUs only).\n* **Forecasting non-BOM SKUs:** Define the logic/approach for predicting demand of SKUs not tied to a BOM.\n* **SKU scope into VDC:** How VDC picks up the in-scope SKUs (likely via an SCC flag).\n* **Vendor scope in Vendor Catalog:** Confirm the relevant vendors are set up (likely already in place).\n* **Vendor parameters:** Capture lead times, desired order cadence, MOQs, and similar buying constraints.\n\nReporting\n---------\n\n[This dashboard](https://looker.wonder.com/dashboards/4959?HDR=&Order+Created+Date=7+day&Accounting+Type=&Accounting+Sub+Type=) is used to track spend on Ed Don orders. This should be used to flag IKCs that place anomalous orders relative to history and peers, suggesting they manage their inventory poorly. This dashboard should join (but denote source) the Platform data with traditional Ed Don source.\n\n---\n\n4. Alternatives Considered\n==========================\n\n|  | Alternative | Description | Why not chosen (this phase) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Keep all ordering on the Ed Don portal** | Status quo: every IKC orders all non-food direct from Ed Don. | Forgoes bulk DISH pricing; keeps ordering outside Pantry; perpetuates 3-way-match / SKU-mapping reconciliation pain. |\n| 2 | **Revive min/max auto-ordering** | The decommissioned Busboy system read Pantry on-hand vs. min/max per item and auto-placed DISH orders nightly. | Min/max is a new pattern for most IKCs (it was sunset before the majority of stores came online) and provides more room for error than the straightforward ordering mechanism stores have today. |\n| 3 | **Order via a separate Supply Chain Portal (not Pantry)** | Build/extend the business-facing SCP for IKC non-food ordering. | GMs already live in Pantry for receiving and inventory; adding a second tool repeats the very fragmentation we're removing. We have planned work to reimagine Pantry Web, and as a result of that effort we may migrate users to SCP. |\n| 4 | **3PL-only distribution (e.g., Extensiv/Shawnee) instead of DISH** | Route non-food bulk through a 3PL WMS rather than DISH/ShipHero. | DISH/ShipHero is the established distribution + inventory source of truth for this flow; 3PL paths exist for specific categories but add integration surface without the cost benefit here. |\n\n---\n\n5. Risks\n========\n\n|  | Risk | Severity | Details | Proposed solution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Item appears in **both** Ed Don guide and Pantry | High | Double-ordering, double-spend, inventory confusion | Enforce mutual exclusivity centrally in SCC; atomic guide-removal on migration; validation/alerting if an item is dual-sourced |\n| 2 | Ledger incompleteness or inaccuracies, and model does not meet expectations | High | Breaks Finance reconciliation; blocks sign-off | Section 3 concerted modeling effort |\n| 3 | Missing SKU cost/value at DISH | High | Repeats $0-PO / 3-way-match failures | Require cost/value on all DISH-sourced non-food SKUs before go-live |\n| 4 | Inaccurate vendor → DISH demand signal (VDC) | High | Over/under-buying into DISH, esp. for non-BOM SKUs with no existing forecast; drives stockouts or excess | Treat VDC as a first-class discovery workstream; combine BOM-based forecasting (MEP) with order-based estimation for non-BOM SKUs; phase in with buffer stock + monitoring |\n| 5 | DISH stockout for a routinely-ordered item | Medium | IKC can't replenish; no vendor fallback once off Ed Don | Pilot with buffer stock; monitor DISH on-hand; defined fallback/rollback to Ed Don sourcing |\n| 6 | Planning not accounting for new DISH demand | Medium | DISH under/over-stocked | **Notify Planning** of the new demand stream even though dev is out of scope |\n| 7 | Scope creep into Planning / full Ed Don migration | Medium | Timeline risk | Hold the line on §1 Non-goals and §2 Out of Scope |\n\n---\n\n6. Open Questions\n=================\n\nWe have a pair of options on modeling this overall solution:\n\nWIP\n\nOrder requests will have race conditions on inventory, uncertainty about how much the store will get, unless we add a reservation logic/availability concept *outside of ShipHero* because the orders haven’t dropped yet so we may need something like Pantry; this allows IKCs to edit their orders until cutoff and we’ll only have one order per store (which is a benefit for DISH). They can still be orders instead of requests, but we just hold them in a draft state (which is fine, totally compatible with system today).\n\nWould we basically just put those orders in a planned state and update availability based on what’s in a planned state? So we’ll need a live inventory service kind of like Pantry? The alternative is that we just create requests and IKCs don’t know if they’ll get stuff if we’re short. It’s not going to be super common that we’re short hopefully, so it’s not like a shot in the dark and they don’t know whether they’ll get stuff, though it’s lower confidence; it doesn’t require a new inventory service though.\n\n|  | Question | Owner | Answer | Due |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Web vs mobile ordering surface in Pantry | Product / Pantry Eng | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 2 | Who are the additional Finance & Accounting sign-off stakeholders beyond Pavel Romanov? | Camden Hill | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 3 | Which non-food SKUs are DISH-eligible (cost-justified) in phase 1? | Merchandising | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 4 | Do we track IKC-level on-hand for these items, or auto-consume on receipt (per item class)? | Product / Finance | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 5 | Ordering cadence: daily, fixed days, or GM-initiated on demand? | Product / DISH Ops | To start, we will run daily routes from DISH to IKC using existing truck routes. We will have an order cutoff so that beyond a certain time of day, the orders have an additional day of lead time. |  |\n| 6 | Are these modeled as 9\\* or 42\\* or something else? | Sadhana Subramanian | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 7 | Of the DISH-eligible SKUs, how many are BOM-linked vs. need order-based demand prediction? | Sim Taylor | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 8 | Can MEP forecast BOM-linked non-food SKUs (currently food-only)? What's required? | Sim Taylor | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 9 | How do we capture vendor parameters (lead times, cadence, MOQs) for vendor → DISH buys? | Sim Taylor / Procurement | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n| 10 | Rollback path if a DISH-sourced item must revert to Ed Don? Would we ever expose direct-to-IKC orders from Ed Don if DISH is OOS of a SKU? | Merchandising | *TBD* | *TBD* |\n\n---\n\n7. Future Potential Enhancements\n================================\n\n|  | Feature | Needed By | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Min/max automated replenishment on the new stack | *TBD* | Re-introduce auto-ordering (the Busboy concept) on Pantry/POMS/ShipHero once GM-entered ordering is proven |\n| 2 | Expand DISH-sourcing to additional non-food categories / vendors | *TBD* | E.g., other 3P vendors currently direct-ordered |\n| 3 | Planning integration for DISH-sourced non-food demand | *TBD* | Move Planning from \"notified\" to \"integrated\" |\n| 4 | Full migration of remaining Ed Don items where cost-justified | *TBD* | Reduce vendor-direct ordering footprint over time |\n\n---\n\n8. Appendix\n===========\n\nSample Query: Shows existing records of 90\\* items getting planned via Ladle\n\nwide760select \\*\nfrom `wonder-recipe-prod.supplychain.brd\\_poms\\_purchase\\_orders\\_e2e`\nWHERE\ndelivery\\_date >= current\\_date()-7 and supplier\\_sku like '90%'\nand supplier.name = 'DISH' and receiver.type = 'HDR'",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "page_width": "default"
  }
}"}

